+NOTES = r"""
+Definition 1.1 fixes Latin and partial Latin squares.  Definitions 1.2 and 1.3
+give the exact local conditions for a Latin bitrade.  Example 1.4 states the
+trade-replacement identity (L minus T) union its mate, Example 1.5 supplies the
+cyclic row-shift construction, and Section 3.1 defines an intercalate as a trade
+of size four.  Section 3 defines B_n as the addition table modulo n.
+
+Step 0 rules out a Track A claim.  The paper proves no hard distribution for
+finding trades.  Its bundled Sage source function is_intercalate checks one
+rectangle directly, while intercalate_homology enumerates all row/column pairs
+with four nested loops.  More strongly, on this module's compact input a machine
+can tabulate the public-to-cyclic row and column permutations and invert them in
+O(qr).  That algorithm produces a witness, so it is reported openly as the Track
+B reference algorithm rather than disguised as a failed attack.
+
+Generation samples a cyclic intercalate before the instance is assembled.  It
+then samples cell-disjoint half-order switch blocks and replaces each trade by
+its mate; these switches preserve the Latin property and every original
+half-order intercalate.  Finally independent Feistel permutations relabel rows,
+columns and symbols and carry the planted trade and mate with them.  The plant
+and every other half-order rectangle have the same distribution.
