Find an intercalate (a size-four Latin trade) and its disjoint mate.

All row, column, and symbol labels are integers from 0 through 15,
inclusive.  The Latin square has order q=16=2^4.  It is given
implicitly by the exact operation L(r,c) defined below; there is no omitted
table and all arithmetic is integer arithmetic.

Map definition.  A Feistel component splits a label v as v=A*m+B with
m=4=2^2 and 0<=A,B<m.  For each displayed [a,b] round,
in the displayed order, replace (A,B) by
    (B, A XOR ((a*B+b) mod m)).
XOR is bitwise exclusive-or.  Recombine as A*m+B.  A chain applies its
components from left to right.  (Generated instances use Feistel components;
the type tag is included to make the representation explicit.)

The three exact map chains are:
  rho   (public row -> cyclic row) = [{"type":"feistel","rounds":[[3,2],[3,2]]}]
  kappa (public column -> cyclic column) = [{"type":"feistel","rounds":[[1,1],[3,1]]}]
  sigma (cyclic symbol -> public symbol) = [{"type":"feistel","rounds":[[1,2],[1,2]]}]

Let h=q/2=8.  Given public labels r,c, set x=rho(r), y=kappa(c),
and z=(x+y) mod q.  Reduce x and y modulo h.  If [x mod h,y mod h]
is one of the following switch coordinates, replace z by (z+h) mod q.
Finally L(r,c)=sigma(z).  The switch list is a set; its order is irrelevant:
  [[0,5],[4,1]]

A partial Latin square is a set of [row,column,symbol] triples with at
most one occurrence of any symbol in each row and each column.  A Latin
bitrade (T,T*) is a pair of partial Latin squares on the same occupied
cells such that their symbols differ in every cell, while each row has
the same symbol set in T and T*, and each column has the same symbol set
in T and T*.  An intercalate is a Latin trade with exactly four entries.

