index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..998bc08d5d94a74576b74fb0fce15e80cef025ac
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,170 @@
+# Latin intercalate generator for arXiv:0907.1481
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | other (exact quasigroup evaluation and trade search) |
+| Certificate | exact symbolic pair of four-entry partial Latin squares |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: half-order differences survive trade switches and isotopy |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+This module turns Carlo Hämäläinen's [*Latin trades and simplicial
+complexes*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1481) into a search for an
+**intercalate**, the paper's name for a Latin trade of size four.  The solver is
+given an exact, compactly represented Latin square: a relabelled back-circulant
+square with a random set of disjoint intercalates switched to their mates.  It
+must return two concrete four-entry partial Latin squares, the trade and its
+disjoint mate.  `verify` evaluates the displayed quasigroup operation and checks
+the partial-Latin, common-support, disjointness, and row/column balance
+conditions directly.  It neither reads the planted answer nor invokes a
+theorem.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+Definitions 1.1–1.3 fix Latin squares and bitrades, Example 1.4 gives the
+trade-replacement identity, Example 1.5 gives the cyclic construction, and
+Section 3.1 defines intercalates.  The bundled Sage code mechanically scans all
+row/column pairs with four nested loops.  The paper provides no hard
+distribution, so a Track A claim would be false.
+
+There is also a better machine algorithm here: tabulate the two displayed
+public-to-cyclic permutations, index the preimages of `0` and `q/2`, and emit
+their rectangle.  It costs `O(q*r)` Feistel rounds and `O(q)` memory.  At the
+shipping preset (`q=4096`, `r=8`, seed 271828) it took 0.005219 seconds and
+65,632 Feistel-round evaluations, about 262,528 primitive exact operations.
+The compressed route is to recognize the half-order invariant and reverse only
+six map evaluations; the conservative count is 205 primitive operations.  This
+machine/mental gap is the Track B claim.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset (seed 0) is hand-solvable.  Its complete rendered instance is:
+
+```text
+Find an intercalate (a size-four Latin trade) and its disjoint mate.
+
+All row, column, and symbol labels are integers from 0 through 15,
+inclusive. The Latin square has order q=16=2^4 and is given by L(r,c).
+
+A Feistel map splits v=A*4+B with 0<=A,B<4. For each [a,b], replace
+(A,B) by (B, A XOR ((a*B+b) mod 4)), then recombine as A*4+B.
+
+rho   = [{"type":"feistel","rounds":[[3,2],[3,2]]}]
+kappa = [{"type":"feistel","rounds":[[1,1],[3,1]]}]
+sigma = [{"type":"feistel","rounds":[[1,2],[1,2]]}]
+
+Let h=8, x=rho(r), y=kappa(c), and z=(x+y) mod 16. If
+[x mod 8,y mod 8] is in [[0,5],[4,1]], replace z by (z+8) mod 16.
+Then L(r,c)=sigma(z).
+
+Return {"trade":[four [row,column,symbol] triples],
+        "mate":[four [row,column,symbol] triples]}.
+Both arrays occupy one complete 2-by-2 rectangle. They are partial Latin
+squares, differ in every cell, and have equal symbol sets in each corresponding
+row and column. The trade entries must equal L at their cells.
+
+Give the JSON object inside <answer></answer> tags.
+```
+
+One answer is:
+
+```json
+{"trade":[[9,8,7],[9,10,13],[11,8,13],[11,10,7]],"mate":[[9,8,13],[9,10,7],[11,8,7],[11,10,13]]}
+```
+
+`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Removing the final trade
+entry returns `(False, "trade must contain exactly four triples")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | bit length `n` | order `q` | Feistel rounds | switches | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 16 | 2 | 2 | hand-scale illustration |
+| easy | 12 | 4,096 | 8 | 32 | current shipping preset; oracle run pending |
+| medium | 14 | 16,384 | 8 | 64 | escalation |
+| hard | 16 | 65,536 | 8 | 96 | escalation |
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses verified |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | prose-tagged and fenced JSON both round-trip |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 guesses; exact fraction 5.9637399e-8 |
+| G5 | pass | 4,194,560 exact shipping solutions in 70,334,388,633,600 candidates; reference 0.005219 s / 65,632 rounds |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks at 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | order 4,096 to 16,384; answer remains 24 atoms |
+| G8 | pass | 100/100 invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 148 characters, about 37 tokens, 24 atoms, 205 intended operations |
+
+The exact shipping count uses a checkable identity: every half-order rectangle
+contributes one intercalate, while each 2-by-2 orbit of switch bits with odd
+parity contributes eight quarter-order rectangles.  Exhaustive enumeration on
+40 small switched squares matched this identity in every case.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The required harness was invoked, but OpenRouter rejected every call with HTTP
+403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`.  The harness correctly recorded these
+as errors, not solver failures, and issued no hardness verdict.
+
+| preset | seed | model | solved | reason |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 817044633 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | error | OpenRouter key limit |
+| easy | 284193663 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | error | OpenRouter key limit |
+| easy | 745098226 | GPT-5.6 Terra | error | OpenRouter key limit |
+| easy | 1643699405 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | error | OpenRouter key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | solved / attempts | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | pending | no valid oracle call |
+| structural hint | pending | no valid oracle call |
+| placebo hint | pending | no valid oracle call |
+
+Accordingly, `hinted - placebo` is not yet measurable.  The local G9 size and
+effort cap passes, but this directory is **not submission-ready until a funded
+OpenRouter key can rerun the bare, structural, and placebo harnesses**.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_0907_1481 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
+text = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer('<answer>' + __import__('json').dumps(inst['answer']) + '</answer>')
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, 'ok')
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit examples with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 0907.1481
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is deliberately Track B.  A machine with ordinary memory solves every
+  instance quickly; the benchmark tests recognition and compressed exact
+  execution, not computational intractability.
+- The 205-operation intended route is below the cap but not tiny.  A no-tool
+  failure may mix failure to notice the invariant with arithmetic error after
+  noticing it; the pending hint/placebo experiment is needed to separate those.
+- `random_candidate` is uniform over unordered row-pair/column-pair rectangles
+  and fills the trade and row-wise mate deterministically.  Its probability is
+  exact for that declared language, but it is not a model of a solver that has
+  inferred the cyclic coordinates.
+- The failed attacks were smallest labels, treating a displayed switch as a
+  public-label outlier, assuming the visible half-shift survives relabelling,
+  and 256 random restarts.  No external SAT, exact-cover, or CAS package was
+  tried; the successful reference algorithm is stronger evidence that tools
+  make the family easy.
+- `canonical_key` is invariant under arbitrary external isotopy maps represented
+  by the module, common-odd-multiplier cyclic affine changes, translations,
+  transpose, and switch ordering.  It uses a valuation histogram and is not a
+  complete canonical form for Latin-square isotopy; exotic isomorphic switch
+  configurations could therefore receive distinct keys.
