Definition 1.1 fixes Latin and partial Latin squares.  Definitions 1.2 and 1.3
give the exact local conditions for a Latin bitrade.  Example 1.4 states the
trade-replacement identity (L minus T) union its mate, Example 1.5 supplies the
cyclic row-shift construction, and Section 3.1 defines an intercalate as a trade
of size four.  Section 3 defines B_n as the addition table modulo n.

Step 0 rules out a Track A claim.  The paper proves no hard distribution for
finding trades.  Its bundled Sage source function is_intercalate checks one
rectangle directly, while intercalate_homology enumerates all row/column pairs
with four nested loops.  More strongly, on this module's compact input a machine
can tabulate the public-to-cyclic row and column permutations and invert them in
O(qr).  That algorithm produces a witness, so it is reported openly as the Track
B reference algorithm rather than disguised as a failed attack.

Generation samples a cyclic intercalate before the instance is assembled.  It
then samples cell-disjoint half-order switch blocks and replaces each trade by
its mate; these switches preserve the Latin property and every original
half-order intercalate.  Finally independent Feistel permutations relabel rows,
columns and symbols and carry the planted trade and mate with them.  The plant
and every other half-order rectangle have the same distribution.

The outlier attack treats a displayed switch coordinate as a public label, the
greedy attack uses the smallest labels, the visible-half-shift attack assumes the
isotopy did nothing, and random restart samples 256 structurally valid
rectangles.  All four are measured on eight shipping seeds.  The intended route
instead notices the half-order invariant and reverses only six short Feistel
evaluations, rather than tabulating the whole permutation.
""".strip()


def _copy_json(value):
    return json.loads(json.dumps(value))


def _feistel(value, rounds, bits, inverse=False):
    """Balanced Feistel permutation of 0..2**bits-1."""

    half_bits = bits // 2
    half = 1 << half_bits
    mask = half - 1
    left, right = divmod(value, half)
    if not inverse:
        for a, b in rounds:
            left, right = right, left ^ ((a * right + b) & mask)
    else:
        for a, b in reversed(rounds):
            left, right = right ^ ((a * left + b) & mask), left
    return left * half + right


def _component(value, component, bits):
    kind = component.get("type")
    inverse = bool(component.get("inverse", False))
    q = 1 << bits
    if kind == "feistel":
        return _feistel(value, component["rounds"], bits, inverse)
    if kind == "affine":
        multiplier = component["multiplier"]
        offset = component["offset"]
        if inverse:
            return ((value - offset) * pow(multiplier, -1, q)) % q
        return (multiplier * value + offset) % q
    raise ValueError("unknown map component")


def _chain(value, components, bits):
    for component in components:
        value = _component(value, component, bits)
    return value


def _inverse_chain(value, components, bits):
    for component in reversed(components):
        inverse = dict(component)
        inverse["inverse"] = not bool(component.get("inverse", False))
        value = _component(value, inverse, bits)
    return value


def _switch_set(inst):
    return {tuple(pair) for pair in inst["switches"]}


def _internal_entry(inst, x, y, switches=None):
    q = inst["order"]
    half = q // 2
    if switches is None:
        switches = _switch_set(inst)
    z = (x + y) % q
    if (x % half, y % half) in switches:
        z = (z + half) % q
    return z


def _entry(inst, row, column, switches=None):
    bits = inst["bits"]
    x = _chain(row, inst["row_map"], bits)
    y = _chain(column, inst["column_map"], bits)
    z = _internal_entry(inst, x, y, switches)
    return _chain(z, inst["symbol_map"], bits)


def _answer_for_rectangle(inst, rows, columns):
    rows = sorted(rows)
    columns = sorted(columns)
    switches = _switch_set(inst)
    r0, r1 = rows
    c0, c1 = columns
    a = _entry(inst, r0, c0, switches)
    b = _entry(inst, r0, c1, switches)
    c = _entry(inst, r1, c0, switches)
    d = _entry(inst, r1, c1, switches)
    trade = [
        [r0, c0, a], [r0, c1, b],
        [r1, c0, c], [r1, c1, d],
    ]
    mate = [
        [r0, c0, b], [r0, c1, a],
        [r1, c0, d], [r1, c1, c],
    ]
    return {"trade": trade, "mate": mate}


def _new_feistel(rng, bits, round_count):
    half = 1 << (bits // 2)
    return {
        "type": "feistel",
        "rounds": [[rng.randrange(half) | 1, rng.randrange(half)]
                   for _ in range(round_count)],
    }


def make_instance(n, seed=0, **params):
    """Construct a relabelled Latin square and a known size-four bitrade.

    ``n`` is the even bit length, so the square order is ``2**n``.  The answer
    is sampled first in cyclic coordinates and carried through all subsequent
