Implemented and locally verified a native Track B Latin-intercalate generator based on Definitions 1.1–1.3 and Section 2.1 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0907.1481).

Files:

- [gen_0907_1481.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0907.1481/gen_0907_1481.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0907.1481/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0907.1481/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0907.1481/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0907.1481/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0907.1481/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

All deterministic gates pass, including:

- G1: 12/12 planted certificates verified.
- G4: 0/200,000 guesses; exact probability `5.9637399e-8`.
- G6: four attacks at 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8.
- G8: 100 invariance checks and 20 distinct instances.
- G9(c): 148 characters, 24 atoms, 205 intended operations.

The result is not submission-ready because every required oracle request returned OpenRouter HTTP 403, “Key limit exceeded (total limit).” Consequently, no valid hardening verdict or G9 comparison could be obtained; the transcripts preserve those errors honestly. A funded OpenRouter key is required to rerun the bare, structural-hint, and placebo arms.
