-# Latin intercalate generator for arXiv:0907.1481
+# Anchored Latin intercalates — arXiv:0907.1481
 
 | profile field | value |
 |---|---|
 | Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
 | Native domain | combinatorics |
 | Object regime | finite discrete |
-| Computational core | other (exact quasigroup evaluation and trade search) |
-| Certificate | exact symbolic pair of four-entry partial Latin squares |
-| Intended intuition | invariant: half-order differences survive trade switches and isotopy |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | exact symbolic four-cell support |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: half-order differences survive every trade switch |
 | Domain essentiality | native |
 | Reduction | none |
 
-**Status:** all deterministic gates pass, but the result is not submission-ready:
-the configured OpenRouter workspace is over its total key limit, so the mandatory
-bare/hinted/placebo oracle calls produced only HTTP 403 errors and no hardness
-verdict.
+This generator turns Carlo Hämäläinen’s [*Latin trades and simplicial
+complexes*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1481) into an anchored-intercalate
+problem.  The solver receives an exact, compactly represented Latin square: a
+back-circulant square, changed by cell-disjoint intercalate switches and
+independently relabelled by reversible xorshift chains.  It must return the four
+cells of the size-four trade through a stated anchor.  The checker evaluates
+the four Latin-square entries and compares the two diagonals exactly; it never
+reads the planted answer.
 
-This module turns Carlo Hämäläinen's [*Latin trades and simplicial
-complexes*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1481) into a search for an
-**intercalate**, the paper's name for a Latin trade of size four.  The solver is
-given an exact, compactly represented Latin square: a relabelled back-circulant
-square with a random set of disjoint intercalates switched to their mates.  It
-must return two concrete four-entry partial Latin squares, the trade and its
-disjoint mate.  `verify` evaluates the displayed quasigroup operation and checks
-the partial-Latin, common-support, disjointness, and row/column balance
-conditions directly.  It neither reads the planted answer nor invokes a
-theorem.
+## Why this is Track B
 
-## Why Track B, not Track A
+Definition 1.1 fixes Latin squares, Definitions 1.2–1.3 fix bitrades, Example
+1.4 licenses replacement of a trade by its mate, Section 2 defines the
+back-circulant square `B_n`, and Section 2.1 defines intercalates.  The paper has
+no hardness theorem or hard distribution, so Track A would be false.  Its Sage
+source uses DLX for mate-finding and four nested loops for its intercalate scan.
 
-Definitions 1.1–1.3 fix Latin squares and bitrades, Example 1.4 gives the
-trade-replacement identity, Example 1.5 gives the cyclic construction, and
-Section 2.1 defines intercalates.  The bundled Sage code uses DLX/exact cover
-to find disjoint mates; its `intercalate_homology` routine scans every
-row-pair/column-pair with four nested loops.  The paper provides no hard
-distribution, so a Track A claim would be false.
+An efficient algorithm for this generated distribution is stated openly.
+Treat each public xorshift chain as a binary linear map and solve its
+half-order preimage with bit-packed Gaussian elimination over GF(2).  The
+implementation uses `O(n^2)` bit-packed row operations, or `O(n^3)` bit
+complexity when the row widths are charged.  At the
+shipping preset (`n=64`, seed 271828), it took **0.002019 s and 23,327 counted
+operations**; across eight seeds it solved 8/8 at 23,115 operations per
+instance.  That is easy for a machine but not executable in context by hand.
 
-The paper's scan examines 70,334,388,633,600 rectangles at the shipping preset.
-There is a much better machine algorithm for this generated distribution:
-tabulate the two displayed public-to-cyclic permutations, index the preimages
-of `0` and `q/2`, and emit their rectangle.  It costs `O(q*r)` Feistel rounds
-and `O(q)` memory.  At the shipping preset (`q=4096`, `r=8`, seed 271828), the
-latest local run took 0.010568 seconds and 65,632 Feistel-round evaluations,
-about 262,528 primitive exact operations.  The compressed route is to recognize
-the half-order invariant and reverse only six map evaluations; the conservative
-count is 205 primitive operations.  This machine/mental gap is the Track B
-claim.
+The compact route notices that adding `q/2` to both hidden cyclic coordinates
+survives every listed switch.  Each map factor is `I+S` for a nilpotent bit
+shift, so `(I+S)^-1 = I+S+S^2+...`; grouping powers by doubling the shift
+distance reverses the chain directly.  At shipping seed 271828 this needs **107
+exact operations**, versus the elimination route’s 23,327.  The generator uses
+that identity by construction; it does not solve its own instance.
 
